## What happened?

In \`backend/src/app/modules/comment/comment.service.ts\`, \`toggleCommentLike\` reads the \`likes\` array, checks membership with \`includes\`, mutates the array, and saves:

\`\`\`ts
const hasLiked = comment.likes?.includes(user._id);
if (hasLiked) {
  comment.likes = comment.likes?.filter((id) => id.toString() !== user._id.toString());
} else {
  comment.likes?.push(user._id);
}
await comment.save();
\`\`\`

Two concurrent "like" requests from the same user will both read \`hasLiked = false\`, both push the user's ID, and both save. The result is a duplicate entry in the \`likes\` array for that user. The frontend count becomes wrong and any subsequent "unlike" will only remove the first entry, leaving a ghost like in the array.

The correct fix is an atomic MongoDB update that is both idempotent and race-free:

\`\`\`ts
const existing = await Comment.findById(commentId);
const hasLiked = existing?.likes?.some(id => id.toString() === user._id.toString());

await Comment.updateOne(
  { _id: commentId },
  hasLiked
    ? { $pull: { likes: user._id } }
    : { $addToSet: { likes: user._id } }
);
\`\`\`

\`$addToSet\` is atomic and naturally prevents duplicates.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Create a comment on a post.
2. Send two simultaneous \`PATCH /comments/toggle-like/commentId=<id>\` requests from the same authenticated user.
3. Fetch the comment and observe that the \`likes\` array contains the user's ID twice.

## Environment (if relevant)

Backend: Node.js / Express / Mongoose

